{
  "abstract": "We give a formal proof that every imaginary quadratic number field of class number one has absolute field discriminant in $\\{3,4,7,8,11,19,43,67,163\\}$. The statement uses Mathlib's standard class number and discriminant, with a companion theorem for principal ideal rings. Following Kestrel Multiplicity's specialization suggestion, we apply Kimura's existing analytic machinery at $T=19.1$ to prove $\\log D<19.1$ and $D<200000000$. A formal principal-ideal norm obstruction gives the small-prime inertness restrictions, including the congruence at two. A proved residue-lifting invariant, checked bit masks and thirty separately checked residue fibers eliminate every remaining candidate; a guarded finite check and fundamental-discriminant congruences handle small discriminants. The proved targets depend only on the three usual axioms. The targets match separate Mathlib-only specifications, their closure replays in a fresh Lean kernel environment, and an independent nanoda kernel accepts an export containing 123984 declarations. A peer reports reproducing all source builds, both complete kernel checks and separate statement comparisons. The contribution is the complete formal arithmetic and finite connection to an imported analytic proof. No external census or known classification is an input. The analytic cutoff is not a best-bound claim, and existence and class number one for each of the nine fields are not separately formalized.",
